{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 974 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "09a8dbeb64bb9e9dabc16f4d804de44ab900e6466dbedd831b4f99d037100603" :edb/db-before-t 973 :edb/db-after-t 974 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534286 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:36:05.145Z" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186203699 1071 "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A = \\{ 2,5,9 \\}.$ \n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (2,2), (2,5), (2,9), (5,9), (9,9) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the relation is symmetric, antisymmetric, both, or neither." 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186203699 1071 "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A = \\{2,5,9 \\}$.\n$R = \\big\\{(2,2), (2,5), (2,9), (5,9), (9,9) \\big\\}$\nDetermine whether the relation is symmetric, antisymmetric, both, or neither." 13194139534286 17592186045417 false] [17592186203699 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, is *symmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (y,x) \\in R$.\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=y$.\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x)$, can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x)$.\n\nOur relation is defined on $\\{2,5,9 \\}$. Notice that:\n\n- $(2,5) \\in R$ and $(5,2) \\notin R$\n- $(2,9) \\in R$ and $(9,2) \\notin R$\n- $(5,9) \\in R$ and $(9,5) \\notin R$\n\nThere are no other pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with\n\n$$\nx \\neq y\n$$\n\nin the relation. So, for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=y\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is antisymmetric." 13194139534286 17592186045417 false] [17592186203699 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (y,x) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nOur relation is defined on $\\{ 2,5,9 \\}.$ Notice that:\n\n- $(2,5) \\in R$ and $(5,2) \\notin R$\n\n- $(2,9) \\in R$ and $(9,2) \\notin R$\n\n- $(5,9) \\in R$ and $(9,5) \\notin R$\n\nThere are no other pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x \\neq y$ in the relation. So, for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is antisymmetric." 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186203700 1071 "![](images/5c/5c7734813ff7d452ee1a8eccb669ef287eb41e6de3e1abe83a58de98c8316fbd.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a relation, defined on the set $A = \\{p,q,r\\},$ that is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric?" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186203700 1071 "![](images/5c/5c7734813ff7d452ee1a8eccb669ef287eb41e6de3e1abe83a58de98c8316fbd.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a relation, defined on the set $A = \\{p,q,r\\}$, that is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric?" 13194139534286 17592186045417 false] [17592186203700 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, is *symmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (y,x) \\in R$.\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x \\rightarrow y$, then the arrow $y \\rightarrow x$ must be in the digraph too.\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=y$.\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x \\rightarrow y$ or $y \\rightarrow x$, can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x \\rightarrow y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y \\rightarrow x$ also belongs to the graph.\n- Digraph II represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x \\neq y$, *at most one* of the arrows (either $x \\rightarrow y$ or $y \\rightarrow x$, but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n- Digraph III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $p \\rightarrow q$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $q \\rightarrow p$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $p \\rightarrow r$ and $r \\rightarrow p$ belong to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534286 17592186045417 false] [17592186203700 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (y,x) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x \\rightarrow y,$ then the arrow $y \\rightarrow x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x \\rightarrow y$ or $y \\rightarrow x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x \\rightarrow y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y \\rightarrow x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph II represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x \\neq y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x \\rightarrow y$ or $y \\rightarrow x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $p \\rightarrow q$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $q \\rightarrow p$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $p \\rightarrow r$ and $r \\rightarrow p$ belong to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186203701 1071 "Which of the following relations are symmetric?\n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $x \\leq y$, defined on $\\mathbb{R}$\n- $x \\: S \\: y$ if and only if $x \\equiv y \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$, defined on $\\mathbb{Z}$\n- $X \\: T \\: Y$ if and only if $X \\cap Y = \\mathbb{N}$, defined on $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$" 13194139534286 17592186045417 false] [17592186203701 1071 "Which of the following relations are symmetric? \n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $x \\leq y,$ defined on $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $x \\: S \\: y$ if and only if $x \\equiv y \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6),$ defined on $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $X \\: T \\: Y$ if and only if $X \\cap Y = \\mathbb{N},$ defined on $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186203701 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, is *symmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (y,x) \\in R$.\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=y$.\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x)$, can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x)$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is not symmetric. Notice that $(0,1) \\in R$ since $0 \\leq 1$, while $(1,0) \\notin R$ since $1 \\not \\leq 0$.\n- Relation $S$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y) \\in S$ then $(y,x) \\in S$. Notice that\n$x \\equiv y \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6) \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y \\equiv x \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$\nfor all $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(X,Y) \\in T$ then $(Y,X) \\in T$. Notice that\n$X \\cap Y = \\mathbb{N} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad Y \\cap X = \\mathbb{N}$\nfor all $X, Y \\in \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ and $T$ only.\"" 13194139534286 17592186045417 false] [17592186203701 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (y,x) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is not symmetric. Notice that \n\n- $(0,1) \\in R$ since $0 \\leq 1,$ while\n\n- $(1,0) \\notin R$ since $1 \\not \\leq 0.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y) \\in S$ then $(y,x) \\in S.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx \\equiv y \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6) \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y \\equiv x \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(X,Y) \\in T$ then $(Y,X) \\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nX \\cap Y = \\mathbb{N} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad Y \\cap X = \\mathbb{N}\n$$\n\nfor all $X, Y \\in \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ and $T$ only.\"" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186203706 1071 "Consider a relation $R$ defined on the set $A = \\{ 1, 4, 9\\}.$ Given that $R$ is symmetric, enter the missing values in the corresponding adjacency matrix below. \n\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ |\n| $9$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186203706 1071 "Consider a relation $R$ defined on the set $A = \\{1, 4, 9\\}$. Given that $R$ is symmetric, enter the missing values in the corresponding adjacency matrix below.\n\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ |\n| $9$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |" 13194139534286 17592186045417 false] [17592186203706 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, is *symmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (y,x) \\in R$.\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, i