n R.$ So, the relation is $\\text{not antisymmetric}.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that the relation is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric." 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186204256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200253 13194139534286 17592186045417 false] [17592186204256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417588 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186204256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186204256 1071 "![](images/fd/fdbcfe164fcca0e005255de10f10c10bedc156f3a582eeb5800322d4c4ebe0ab.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent symmetric relations defined on the set $A = {0, 2, 4}$?" 13194139534286 17592186045417 false] [17592186204256 1071 "![](images/fd/fdbcfe164fcca0e005255de10f10c10bedc156f3a582eeb5800322d4c4ebe0ab.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent symmetric relations defined on the set $A={0,2,4}?$" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186204256 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/9c/9c9c16300cfb95a61a261e39db865d5c6d056794bd7aaa422567bde10dd4abf5.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(4,2)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(2,4)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(0,4)$ and $(4,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186204259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200280 13194139534286 17592186045417 false] [17592186204259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200284 13194139534286 17592186045417 false] [17592186204259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200288 13194139534286 17592186045417 false] [17592186204259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417566 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186204259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417569 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186204259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417594 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186204259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186204259 1071 "Consider a relation $R$ that is defined on the set $A = {1, 2, 3}$. Given that $R$ is antisymmetric, enter the missing values in the adjacency matrix.\n\n**Note**: If there is not enough information to determine a particular cell's value, then select the question mark $(?)$.\n\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ | $0$ |" 13194139534286 17592186045417 false] [17592186204259 1071 "Consider a relation $R$ that is defined on the set $A={1,2,3}.$ Given that $R$ is antisymmetric, enter the missing values in the adjacency matrix. \n\n**Note**: If there is not enough information to determine a particular cell's value, then select the question mark $(\\text{?}).$\n\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ | $0$ |" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186204259 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A b