 defined on $R$\n\n- $X\\,T\\,Y$ if and only if $|X|=|Y|,$ defined on $P(N)$" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417602 1072 #uuid "16c25cec-ba90-5814-9c8d-34f1bb30c2b1" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417602 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in R$ then $(y,x)\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟹\\,y≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in S$ then $(y,x)\\in S.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⟹\\,y+x=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in R.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(X,Y)\\in T$ then $(Y,X)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n|X|=|Y|\\,⟹\\,|Y|=|X|\n$$\n\nfor all $X,Y\\in P(N).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$, $S,$ and $T.$\"" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417550 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417577 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417595 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417603 1069 "q-213473" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417603 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417603 1071 "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A={0,2,5}.$ \n\n$$\nR={(0,0),(0,2),(5,0),(5,2