st one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $1\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 1$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 3$ and $3\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417552 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417568 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417581 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417613 1069 "q-213594" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417613 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417613 1071 "Let $R$ be a relation defined on the set $R$ as follows: \n\n$x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x\\le y$ \n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⟹\\,{{field-2}},\n$$\n\nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}." 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417613 1072 #uuid "ceafef3e-b20d-5064-9135-0687709767da" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417613 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nThe relation $R$ is antisymmetric. Indeed, for any real numbers $x$ and $y,$ if $x\\le y$ and $y\\le x,$ then $x$ and $y$ must be the same number.\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\nx\\le y\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\le x\\,⟹\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nthe relation is $\\text{antisymmetric}.$\n\n**Note:** $R$ is not symmetric since, for example, $0\\le 1$ but $1≰0.$" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417559 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417563 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417564 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417573 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417614 1069 "q-213493" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417614 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417614 1071 "Consider a relation $R$ that is defined on the set $A={2,4,6}.$ Given that $R$ is symmetric, enter the missing values in the adjacency matrix. \n\n**Note**: If there is not enough information to determine a particular cell's value, then select the question mark $(\\text{?}).$\n\n|  | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n| $6$ | {{field-4}} | $1$ | $0$ |" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417614 1072 #uuid "d4393a9f-4375-593d-841f-a618d5e585c3" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417614 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWe are given elements of the adjacency matrix in the following highlighted cells outside the diagonal:  \n\n|  | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  | $0$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ |  |  |\n| $6$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nAs a result, the relation contains the pairs $(4,2)$ and $(6,4).$ Since $R$ must be symmetric, we require \n\n$$\n(2,4)\\in R,\\,(4,6)\\in R.\n$$\n\nSo, the cells corresponding to these elements must all equal $1.$\n\n|  | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ |  | $1$ |\n| $6$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nAlso, note the following:\n\n- The symmetric relation condition is equivalent to $(x,y)\\notin R⟹(y,x)\\notin R.$ Therefore, $(2,6)\\notin R$ implies $(6,2)\\notin R.$\n\n- We do not have enough information to determine whether $(4,4)$ is an element of $R.$\n\nLet's add this information to our table.\n\n|  | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $?$ | $1$ |\n| $6$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417587 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417615 1069 "q-213540" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417615 1071 "![](images/70/70743ac83b876995835703b35fc133af2133960ea916f589d60c8ac5636ee4ef.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a symmetric relation defined on the set $A={a,b,c}?$" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417615 1072 #uuid "ee6fe66d-09a5-518d-9a32-a533c9b3cc75" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417615 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph II represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417580 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417616 1069 "q-213600" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417616 1071 "Which of the following relations are symmetric? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $y={x}^{2},$ defined on $R$\n\n- $X\\,S\\,Y$ if and only if $X⊂Y,$ defined on $P(R)$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $4∣(x+y),$ defined on $N$" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417616 1072 #uuid "fe8a5755-a006-512f-a829-ac55db45a0f7" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true] [17592186417616 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is not symmetric. Notice that \n\n- $(-1,1)\\in R$ since $1=(-1)^{2},$ while\n\n- $(1,-1)\\notin R$ since $-1\\ne {1}^{2}.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is not symmetric. Notice that \n\n- $({1},Z)\\in S$ since ${1}⊂Z,$ while\n\n- $(Z,{1})\\notin S$ since $Z⊄{1}.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in T$ then $(y,x)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n4∣(x+y)\\,⟹\\,4∣(y+x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\"" 13194139534286 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-03d90713-bab4-5982-9789-c8022a2a41da" 17592186417380 "answer-03e361b5-5d8f-54c1-8b56-56d87b031bc6" 17592186417381 "answer-07e823f2-bcb9-5505-9e22-c4765433fa1a" 17592186417382 "answer-0e2e1750-3caa-5b3d-af7f-d0ad174576a7" 17592186417383 "answer-107952e7-e54f-5bd6-a15c-561e0fb50805" 17592186417384 "answer-1281ef50-1c43-5486-95a6-9274432d97a0" 17592186417385 "answer-12c5373d-0629-56d7-901e-7af879dd9ae2" 17592186417386 "answer-12ca0759-a9dd-50c8-8c4d-d68d2d81373a" 17592186417387 "answer-1366d776-b1cf-5d05-ba47-1198ddc13169" 17592186417388 "answer-15596502-16d6-5f2a-842b-48e247cfb1d2" 17592186417389 "answer-19eae037-9bb3-53cd-9405-bcdcf90085f1" 17592186417390 "answer-1a69a6c5-e5bb-5333-b9a5-93cc8a4905a2" 17592186417391 "answer-1dc38fe7-4cc0-5b89-838a-99b8f54f414a" 17592186417392 "answer-1fc5dc30-0af1-522a-98a7-9dc7cae28dc1" 17592186417393 "answer-1fe57770-3449-5f3e-8961-ce07a19a78c9" 17592186417394 "answer-2020